            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use Pythagoras’ theorem to find the height of an equilateral face, then add the areas of the four congruent faces.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let each edge be $x$ cm and the perpendicular height of one triangular face be $h$ cm. Each face is equilateral, so its altitude bisects its base.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h^2&=x^2-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2\\\\h^2&=\\frac{3x^2}{4}\\\\h&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{2}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThere are four congruent triangular faces.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}S&=4\\times\\frac12\\times x\\times\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{2}\\\\S&=\\sqrt{3}x^2\\\\64\\sqrt{3}&=\\sqrt{3}x^2\\\\x^2&=64\\\\x&=8\\end{align*}$$\n\nEach edge is $8\\text{ cm}$ long.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
